Cluster-monitoring Grafana dashboard (timelakedb#117)
2026-08-28 · router + ingester×2 + querier×2 + compactor + localstack, with the
             Prometheus + Grafana monitoring overlay
             (timelakedb-cluster-s3.yml + timelakedb-cluster-s3.monitoring.yml)
   dashboard: deploy/grafana/cluster/dashboards/timelakedb-cluster.json
   drill:     deploy/compose/cluster-drill/cluster_dashboard_drill.sh
================================================================================

THE CLAIM UNDER TEST is that the cluster dashboard has real, per-node data to
draw. It is asserted against PROMETHEUS, not Grafana pixels: the panels are
PromQL, so if the query a panel is built on returns the series, the panel draws
it — and this is the honest check, because a panel reads "green" just as easily
when its query is wrong and returns nothing successfully.

Prometheus scrapes every node's /metrics (NOT the _system Flight SQL path,
which is down exactly when the cluster view is needed) and relabels each target
by node + role. Four legs:

  - Tiers populate. up=6; ingester lines/s on BOTH ingesters (the shard hash
    spread the writes); router forwarding; querier serving; CL-2 replication;
    an object-store put, i.e. a flush actually landed.
  - Provisioning. Grafana loaded the dashboard and the Prometheus datasource,
    both resolved by uid.
  - Convergence. Spread is 0 with every node level, then flips to 1 the instant
    one node's applied config is advanced (#109 config_revision) — the panel
    that flags a node held behind, the same fact the U3 console view shows.
  - CL-2 degraded. Kill an ingester and its peer's timelake_cl2_degraded lights
    up (PR-7); up{role="ingester"} drops to 1.

WHAT THE METRICS ACTUALLY EXPOSE, confirmed here and reflected in the panels
(the exposition is per-role — a panel must tolerate a role missing a series,
never read it as zero):
  - config_revision is on all five engine nodes; the router holds no engine
    and has none, so the convergence math is over five, not six.
  - catalog_head is on the log FOLLOWERS (queriers + compactor); ingesters, the
    writers, do not expose it yet — filed as a follow-up, not faked in a query.
    (#123, same day: catalog_head now ships on every engine node, the writers
    included; the drill gained a count==5 check for it. This transcript
    predates that change — the metric is pinned by the unit test
    `a_writer_emits_its_committed_catalog_head`.)
  - compactions_total ticks on the ingesters (they compact their own files);
    the by-node panels attribute it honestly rather than assuming a tier.

